WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.485 --> 00:00:04.386
- I didn't come in with the
opportunity to play right away.

00:00:04.386 --> 00:00:05.312
I had to earn it.

00:00:05.312 --> 00:00:07.901
And it was hard, I remember taking a walk

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:09.863
with my dad and mom around the block.

00:00:09.863 --> 00:00:10.931
I have a lot of fun, I mean

00:00:10.931 --> 00:00:12.696
I get to play football for a living,

00:00:12.696 --> 00:00:14.089
I mean what could be any better than that?

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:17.237
When I'm playing
football, I appreciate it.

00:00:17.237 --> 00:00:19.165
I'm nowhere else, I'm in the present.

00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:21.522
To me, what separates really good players

00:00:21.522 --> 00:00:24.517
from great players, execute
well under pressure.

00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.748
As long as you believe
in what you're doing,

00:00:25.748 --> 00:00:27.419
and you're doing things you love to do,

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:29.637
then you can go out there
and be the best at it.

00:00:29.637 --> 00:00:33.804
My family is, you know one,
and then football is one A.

00:00:34.916 --> 00:00:36.182
When I watch myself play at times,

00:00:36.182 --> 00:00:37.912
I still don't think I'm very good.

00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:39.177
I've got to do things differently,

00:00:39.177 --> 00:00:40.640
and I've got to evolve over time.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.985
Stay hungry, stay humble, on three.

00:00:42.985 --> 00:00:45.133
- He's an American football quarterback

00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:46.886
for the New England Patriots.

00:00:46.886 --> 00:00:49.394
In his 13 seasons in the
National Football League,

00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:52.831
he's been to the Super Bowl
six times, and won four.

00:00:52.831 --> 00:00:54.270
He is considered among the greatest

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.720
NFL quarterbacks of all time.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.389
He's Tom Brady, and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:03.300
(dramatic swoosh)

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.458
- I didn't have an easy experience.

00:01:06.458 --> 00:01:09.401
I didn't have, come in
as a top rated recruit.

00:01:09.401 --> 00:01:13.848
I didn't come in with the
opportunity to play right away.

00:01:13.848 --> 00:01:15.868
I had to earn it.

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:17.099
And you know what greatest honor

00:01:17.099 --> 00:01:20.082
I've ever received as a player is?

00:01:20.082 --> 00:01:22.300
In my fourth year, and my fifth year

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.796
I was named team captain.

00:01:24.796 --> 00:01:29.475
That, to this day, is the
single greatest achievement

00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:32.415
I've ever had as a football player.

00:01:32.415 --> 00:01:36.281
Because the men in this room
chose me, to lead their team.

00:01:36.281 --> 00:01:37.790
And these were my best friends.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.319
These were the guys that, that
knew that I liked to work,

00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:42.922
that knew that I love football,

00:01:42.922 --> 00:01:45.302
that knew that I love to play.

00:01:45.302 --> 00:01:49.864
That knew that I wanted to be
the quarterback for Michigan.

00:01:49.864 --> 00:01:53.057
And all the lessons that I
learned here on State Street,

00:01:53.057 --> 00:01:55.681
and in the big house, that's still

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:58.142
what I bring to practice today.

00:01:58.142 --> 00:02:00.070
And after 14 years, I love the game

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.881
more than I've ever loved it.

00:02:01.881 --> 00:02:03.843
But where did I learn
the love for the game?

00:02:03.843 --> 00:02:05.039
Where did I learn to practice?

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:07.256
Where did I learn to compete?

00:02:07.256 --> 00:02:09.590
It was sitting in the same chairs,

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.227
that you guys are sitting in today.

00:02:11.227 --> 00:02:13.409
(dramatic music)

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:14.640
- [Announcer] Tom pulls
away, play fake to the Lewis,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.789
stands in, he pumps left,

00:02:15.789 --> 00:02:19.319
he throws to the end
zone, touchdown Amendola.

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:21.736
And number 400 for Tom Brady.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:24.114
- [Announcer] He becomes
the fourth quarterback

00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:28.281
in NFL history, to reach the
400 touchdown passing plateau.

00:02:30.105 --> 00:02:32.145
- We were lead to believe that
he was going to be drafted,

00:02:32.145 --> 00:02:35.227
possibly second round,
probably third round.

00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:37.189
They kept calling quarterback names,

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:39.272
and we kept being stoned.

00:02:40.521 --> 00:02:42.739
- You see, you know, so-and-so get picked,

00:02:42.739 --> 00:02:45.571
and then there's nine more picks gone

00:02:45.571 --> 00:02:49.716
until one team that you
think was interested.

00:02:49.716 --> 00:02:52.096
You know that's, you get pretty anxious.

00:02:52.096 --> 00:02:54.070
- We were very distraught.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.356
With each name it was
becoming worse and worse.

00:02:57.356 --> 00:02:59.283
- And so I remember being downstairs

00:02:59.283 --> 00:03:02.777
for like the fourth round,
and that came and gone.

00:03:02.777 --> 00:03:05.517
And then the fifth round
was coming and going,

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.770
and all these other guys
were getting picked.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.757
- The beginning of the sixth round,

00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:10.637
he says, "I got to get out of here."

00:03:10.637 --> 00:03:15.142
So he gets up, comes downstairs,
and grabs a baseball bat

00:03:15.142 --> 00:03:17.325
and starts walking up the street.

00:03:17.325 --> 00:03:18.428
- You know I don't want to sit around,

00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:20.599
and make my hopes get up, or

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:23.524
I'd rather just take my mind off of it.

00:03:23.524 --> 00:03:25.637
- And finally, he comes
back into the house,

00:03:25.637 --> 00:03:29.637
and it's about the 22nd
pick of the sixth round.

00:03:30.804 --> 00:03:33.521
And he says, "I got to get out of here."

00:03:33.521 --> 00:03:37.932
The poor guy, the tension in
the house is just really rough.

00:03:37.932 --> 00:03:40.510
- And it was hard, I
remember taking a walk

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.593
with my dad and mom around the block.

00:03:51.899 --> 00:03:53.982
(sniffs)

00:03:56.311 --> 00:03:59.086
It was just a tough day, you know.

00:03:59.086 --> 00:04:03.086
I just remember being
there with my mom and dad.

00:04:03.997 --> 00:04:07.526
(sniffs) Sorry about that.

00:04:07.526 --> 00:04:10.301
You know they just were
so supportive of me.

00:04:10.301 --> 00:04:13.935
And they take it as emotional as I do.

00:04:13.935 --> 00:04:17.499
And you know finally
when the Patriots called,

00:04:17.499 --> 00:04:19.844
I was so excited, you know, I was like

00:04:19.844 --> 00:04:23.026
I don't have be an insurance
salesman, you know.

00:04:23.026 --> 00:04:26.683
So, my family was there,
we were all excited.

00:04:26.683 --> 00:04:29.922
And thank God I got picked here.

00:04:29.922 --> 00:04:31.339
- He got drafted.

00:04:32.755 --> 00:04:34.505
And we were jubilant.

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:39.568
(dramatic music)

00:04:40.719 --> 00:04:42.972
- [Announcer] Brady, steps up

00:04:42.972 --> 00:04:46.556
Brady on the move, pump, Brady
looking for the end zone.

00:04:46.556 --> 00:04:49.098
Brady, did he get there?

00:04:49.098 --> 00:04:52.547
- [Announcer] And he's going
to be marked out inside the one

00:04:52.547 --> 00:04:54.033
- [Announcer] Any
questions about the ankle?

00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:56.459
- [Announcer] Brady's
jacking up McDaniels.

00:04:56.459 --> 00:04:58.549
They got Brady with Josh McDaniels.

00:04:58.549 --> 00:05:00.720
- [Announcer] Listen to Brady cheer.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.241
- What, what keeps you going, man?

00:05:02.241 --> 00:05:05.968
- I think it's, there's
nothing I'd rather do so,

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:07.733
I don't have a lot of hobbies you know,

00:05:07.733 --> 00:05:10.382
I don't, you know I play
golf every once in awhile.

00:05:10.382 --> 00:05:13.168
But there's something in my
DNA that loves this game.

00:05:13.168 --> 00:05:15.548
And I just, I never want it to go away.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.151
You know, my goal is to play, you know

00:05:17.151 --> 00:05:18.509
because I love the game, and I'm not

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:20.227
I don't really plan on
doing anything else.

00:05:20.227 --> 00:05:21.702
And I want to play, then you know

00:05:21.702 --> 00:05:22.572
what else do I have to do today?

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:24.453
It is my job to take care
of myself year round.

00:05:24.453 --> 00:05:26.253
So, you know, to eat the right things,

00:05:26.253 --> 00:05:29.945
to drink the right things,
to keep my body pliable,

00:05:29.945 --> 00:05:30.778
the way it needs to be

00:05:30.778 --> 00:05:33.056
to sustain the hits, to make the throws.

00:05:33.056 --> 00:05:34.902
You know, to keep my brain
working the right way.

00:05:34.902 --> 00:05:36.911
I mean all those things,
that's what I think about

00:05:36.911 --> 00:05:40.231
in February, that's what
I think about in August,

00:05:40.231 --> 00:05:41.671
that's what I think about in November.

00:05:41.671 --> 00:05:43.389
So, you know I just love playing,

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:45.316
I don't want it to go away anytime soon.

00:05:45.316 --> 00:05:46.640
You know, be in a situation where

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.393
they say, well you're 37,
and I'm still thinking

00:05:48.393 --> 00:05:50.076
37, I feel like I'm 27.

00:05:50.076 --> 00:05:52.375
You know and I just want to keep doing it.

00:05:52.375 --> 00:05:53.687
So, I have a lot of fun,

00:05:53.687 --> 00:05:55.580
we get to play football for a living.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.426
I mean what could be any better than that?

00:05:57.426 --> 00:05:59.225
(dramatic music)

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:01.686
- [Announcer] As Brady again under attack,

00:06:01.686 --> 00:06:04.519
steps out of it and is
able to get it to Edelman

00:06:04.519 --> 00:06:07.387
who's getting them in field goal range.

00:06:07.387 --> 00:06:08.943
- [Announcer] Well look,
that's all Tom Brady there.

00:06:08.943 --> 00:06:10.951
No question, said to him last night,

00:06:10.951 --> 00:06:12.832
"You know, you're moving around

00:06:12.832 --> 00:06:14.306
"more than I've seen you move."

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:16.269
And he goes, "Yep, I'm
watching the other quarterbacks

00:06:16.269 --> 00:06:18.811
"in the league, I'm studying
them, they're moving."

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.657
So he has been moving, of course

00:06:20.657 --> 00:06:22.491
he moves to throw the football.

00:06:22.491 --> 00:06:25.817
- When I'm playing
football, I appreciate it.

00:06:25.817 --> 00:06:27.710
I'm nowhere else, I'm in the present.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.962
It's probably the best part about,

00:06:29.962 --> 00:06:32.551
best part about my life, is
that to be in the present,

00:06:32.551 --> 00:06:34.060
you talk about how do you enjoy your life,

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.860
how do you find balance, right?

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.044
It's to live in the present.

00:06:37.044 --> 00:06:40.006
To live like a kid, like why
do kids have so much fun?

00:06:40.006 --> 00:06:42.804
Because they just care
about what they're doing.

00:06:42.804 --> 00:06:45.335
And if we thought about what
we were doing all the time,

00:06:45.335 --> 00:06:47.506
and you do that when you're
on the football field.

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:49.270
It's probably the only place
that I can really do that,

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:51.976
when I really truly am my authentic self.

00:06:51.976 --> 00:06:55.215
I mean a lot of people see it,
my close, close friends do,

00:06:55.215 --> 00:06:56.190
but when I'm on the football field,

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.291
there's no, it's just raw.

00:06:58.291 --> 00:07:00.300
(dramatic music)

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.322
- [Announcer] Takes the snap, looks right,

00:07:01.322 --> 00:07:03.911
throws it for White, touchdown Patriots.

00:07:03.911 --> 00:07:05.350
- [Announcer] Wow was that easy

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:08.815
- [Announcer] Career
touchdown pass, number 421,

00:07:08.815 --> 00:07:11.485
Tom Brady now is third all time

00:07:11.485 --> 00:07:14.109
in that category, all by his lonesome.

00:07:14.109 --> 00:07:15.380
- You know enough, it ain't over yet.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:16.505
- [Smith] They got to give us
another, the got to give us

00:07:16.505 --> 00:07:18.920
- Not with that dude,
with that quarterback.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.207
- To me what separates really good players

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:24.934
from great players, execute
well under pressure.

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:27.070
The biggest games, the greatest stage,

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.694
and I think that's what playing
quarterback is all about.

00:07:29.694 --> 00:07:30.527
(crowd cheering)

00:07:30.527 --> 00:07:33.844
- [Announcer] A pulse pounding
finish to Super Bowl 38.

00:07:33.844 --> 00:07:38.686
Snap, ball down, kick up, kick
is on the way, kick is good.

00:07:38.686 --> 00:07:41.681
Kick is good, and it's over.

00:07:41.681 --> 00:07:45.289
They're the Super Bowl champions again.

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:47.379
(dramatic music)

00:07:47.379 --> 00:07:48.902
- [Announcer] Chatham snap to
Brady, White out of the back,

00:07:48.902 --> 00:07:50.794
Tom steps up under pressure, lifting it,

00:07:50.794 --> 00:07:52.520
lobs the throw, down the
right side for Chandler.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.215
Jump ball, makes the catch at the 10.

00:07:54.215 --> 00:07:56.827
Driven to the near
sideline by two defenders,

00:07:56.827 --> 00:07:59.416
and forced out at the six yard line.

00:07:59.416 --> 00:08:00.693
What an effort by Brady,

00:08:00.693 --> 00:08:02.911
to keep the play alive in the pocket.

00:08:02.911 --> 00:08:03.886
- [Announcer] Brady put it in a spot

00:08:03.886 --> 00:08:05.198
where he could go up and get it.

00:08:05.198 --> 00:08:08.309
- I think you know a lot
it is believing yourself

00:08:08.309 --> 00:08:09.540
and believing what you're capable.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.001
And I think as you grow up, there's a lot

00:08:12.001 --> 00:08:14.416
of people along the way
that will support you.

00:08:14.416 --> 00:08:17.330
And there's, you know
as long as you believe

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:19.466
in what you're doing, and you're
doing things you love to do

00:08:19.466 --> 00:08:21.846
then you can go out there
and be the best at it.

00:08:21.846 --> 00:08:23.820
And that's, you know that
should give you a lot of

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.898
enjoyment, fulfillment in your life.

00:08:25.898 --> 00:08:27.779
(dramatic music)

00:08:27.779 --> 00:08:29.290
- [Announcer] Block in the
back, play fake to LeGarrette,

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:31.206
Tom's standing, he's going
to go deep down the left side

00:08:31.206 --> 00:08:33.005
for LaFell, open inside the 30.

00:08:33.005 --> 00:08:36.349
LaFell has the catch,
spins away to the inside

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:38.612
and he's dropped at the 15 yard line.

00:08:38.612 --> 00:08:42.130
- [Announcer] What a play call,
and then just going downtown

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.380
- You're a father now, three kids.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.131
Has that made you a better player?

00:08:47.131 --> 00:08:49.511
- I would say it's made
me a better teammate.

00:08:49.511 --> 00:08:53.679
It's an evolution as person,
and I think my appreciation

00:08:53.679 --> 00:08:56.547
for the game is at the
highest it's ever been.

00:08:56.547 --> 00:08:58.625
And I think because the priorities,

00:08:58.625 --> 00:08:59.647
you know you have your priorities

00:08:59.647 --> 00:09:00.796
and you make your priorities.

00:09:00.796 --> 00:09:04.963
So my family is, you know one,
and then football is one A.

00:09:06.555 --> 00:09:08.517
You know, and when it's
family time, it's family time,

00:09:08.517 --> 00:09:11.257
and when it's football time,
it's football time, and

00:09:11.257 --> 00:09:12.731
(dramatic music)

00:09:12.731 --> 00:09:14.658
- [Announcer] Brady under
center, play fake to Blount,

00:09:14.658 --> 00:09:15.970
deep drop by Thompson, steps up,

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.445
throws down the left
side for Rob Gronkowski,

00:09:17.445 --> 00:09:19.778
he makes the drive at the 45,

00:09:19.778 --> 00:09:22.692
driven to the nearest
sideline by Bacarri Rambo.

00:09:22.692 --> 00:09:26.593
Rambo says it's incomplete,
Gronkowski has it

00:09:26.593 --> 00:09:28.799
for a first down New England.

00:09:28.799 --> 00:09:31.957
- [Announcer] What a strike
by Brady in the pocket.

00:09:31.957 --> 00:09:34.744
He drops it in, over top of coverage.

00:09:34.744 --> 00:09:37.202
- [Announcer] Brady hangs in
with a strike to Gronkowski.

00:09:37.202 --> 00:09:39.094
- [Voiceover] A decade into his career,

00:09:39.094 --> 00:09:42.786
the man taken with pick
199 has achieved it all.

00:09:42.786 --> 00:09:46.508
But still feels he has plenty to prove.

00:09:46.508 --> 00:09:48.064
- It's not really a chip on my shoulder,

00:09:48.064 --> 00:09:52.000
it's just that feeling that,
man, maybe nobody want ya.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:53.149
When I watch myself play at times,

00:09:53.149 --> 00:09:55.402
I still don't think I'm very good.

00:09:55.402 --> 00:09:56.806
Man, you're still not very fast,

00:09:56.806 --> 00:09:59.059
you know you got to decent arm, you know,

00:09:59.059 --> 00:10:02.368
you made some pretty
bad reads on that day.

00:10:02.368 --> 00:10:04.504
(triumphant music)

00:10:04.504 --> 00:10:07.165
That's what gets me up and motivates me.

00:10:07.165 --> 00:10:08.396
I always want to feel like

00:10:08.396 --> 00:10:11.391
I'm the best quarterback for this team.

00:10:11.391 --> 00:10:14.549
I want to earn it every single day.

00:10:14.549 --> 00:10:16.488
(dramatic music)

00:10:16.488 --> 00:10:18.287
- [Announcer] Second down and
20 for the Pats, from the 39.

00:10:18.287 --> 00:10:19.762
Brady with a hitch and
a throw to the left,

00:10:19.762 --> 00:10:23.163
and it's caught by LaFell
at the 20 yard line.

00:10:23.163 --> 00:10:26.509
Little by the wire, but a laser by Brady.

00:10:26.509 --> 00:10:29.794
Just beyond the outstretched
hands of Darrelle Revis,

00:10:29.794 --> 00:10:31.650
into Brandon LaFell,

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.243
and first catch of the
season for 19 yards.

00:10:34.243 --> 00:10:36.252
- [Announcer] That's a tight
window to throw that ball into.

00:10:36.252 --> 00:10:37.982
I though that thing was
going to get picked.

00:10:37.982 --> 00:10:40.164
- Well everyone has their
different belief systems,

00:10:40.164 --> 00:10:43.812
and I think everyone has
their different opinions of

00:10:43.812 --> 00:10:45.795
you know those types of
things that you talked about.

00:10:45.795 --> 00:10:47.723
Certainly as an athlete, I care very much

00:10:47.723 --> 00:10:48.988
what I put into my body.

00:10:48.988 --> 00:10:52.599
I think that, I'm 38 years
old, I'm not 22 any more.

00:10:52.599 --> 00:10:54.480
- You look good

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.198
- You're very sweet thanks, but I

00:10:56.198 --> 00:10:57.591
you know all those things are important.

00:10:57.591 --> 00:10:59.588
And all the little things
add up to be big things.

00:10:59.588 --> 00:11:02.955
So when I'm trying to accomplish
things professionally that

00:11:02.955 --> 00:11:05.381
you know in a lot of ways,
I'm in uncharted territory.

00:11:05.381 --> 00:11:07.401
So I've got to do things differently,

00:11:07.401 --> 00:11:08.911
and I've got to evolve over time.

00:11:08.911 --> 00:11:11.001
I've got to, things that
may have worked in the past

00:11:11.001 --> 00:11:12.603
maybe don't work as you move forward.

00:11:12.603 --> 00:11:14.367
So, the lessons that I've learned

00:11:14.367 --> 00:11:15.761
through my own trial and error,

00:11:15.761 --> 00:11:16.910
I think I'm at that point in my career

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.419
where I'm very efficient
with what I'm doing.

00:11:18.419 --> 00:11:20.022
So I try to eat healthy, I try to

00:11:20.022 --> 00:11:21.055
get the right amount of rest,

00:11:21.055 --> 00:11:23.098
I try to do the right types of exercise

00:11:23.098 --> 00:11:25.107
so I can be on the field
and be available to my team.

00:11:25.107 --> 00:11:26.964
I try to be, you know, as productive

00:11:26.964 --> 00:11:29.344
this time in my career as I was earlier.

00:11:29.344 --> 00:11:31.434
(dramatic music)

00:11:31.434 --> 00:11:33.001
- [Announcer] Snap to Brady, standing,

00:11:33.001 --> 00:11:34.813
throws down the right seam for Gronkowski.

00:11:34.813 --> 00:11:38.412
He makes the catch at the
15, tumbling to the 10.

00:11:38.412 --> 00:11:41.697
Double covered, he
still has the reception.

00:11:41.697 --> 00:11:44.077
And there is still life for the Patriots.

00:11:44.077 --> 00:11:45.807
- [Announcer] I can't
believe what I just saw.

00:11:45.807 --> 00:11:48.687
And Gronkowski is having
a tough time getting up.

00:11:48.687 --> 00:11:51.879
He runs a go route
through double coverage,

00:11:51.879 --> 00:11:55.208
the inside seam, and Brady
lays it out on fourth and 10.

00:11:55.208 --> 00:11:56.599
(dramatic music)

00:11:56.599 --> 00:11:58.770
- [Announcer] Direct snap to
Brady, stands in the pocket,

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.115
fires it to the right,
down the right sideline,

00:12:01.115 --> 00:12:05.282
and caught, that's at 20, 15,
10, five, touchdown, Patriots

00:12:06.246 --> 00:12:10.706
For Tom Brady, touchdown pass
number 50, an NFL record.

00:12:10.706 --> 00:12:14.793
- [Announcer] Tom Brady has
thrown five touchdown passes.

00:12:14.793 --> 00:12:16.395
All here in the second quarter.

00:12:16.395 --> 00:12:18.079
- That was fun

00:12:18.079 --> 00:12:20.474
- We've never had one of those.

00:12:20.474 --> 00:12:22.583
We never had one.

00:12:22.583 --> 00:12:25.676
- [Announcer] And Foxboro loves Tom Brady.

00:12:25.676 --> 00:12:29.032
- [Announcer] Because let's
face it, he's the franchise.

00:12:29.032 --> 00:12:31.949
(triumphant music)

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:37.623
- Hey, stay hungry, stay humble, on three.

00:12:37.623 --> 00:12:38.807
One, two, three

00:12:38.807 --> 00:12:40.560
- [All Players] Stay hungry, stay humble

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.393
(dramatic music)

00:12:43.393 --> 00:12:45.111
- [Announcer] Snap directly
to James White he runs left,

00:12:45.111 --> 00:12:46.307
shovels it back to Danny Amendola,

00:12:46.307 --> 00:12:47.248
he's going to throw it to Brady

00:12:47.248 --> 00:12:49.709
who makes the catch at
the 35, runs to the 50,

00:12:49.709 --> 00:12:52.292
45, 40, Tom gets out of bounds.

00:12:53.529 --> 00:12:56.524
A gadget play, direct snap to White,

00:12:56.524 --> 00:13:00.344
shovel back to Amendola, who
throws the completion to Brady,

00:13:00.344 --> 00:13:02.515
to the 39 of Philadelphia.

00:13:02.515 --> 00:13:05.515
- [Announcer] I feel all tingly now.

00:13:06.381 --> 00:13:07.449
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:07.449 --> 00:13:09.376
I hope you enjoyed, I
made this video because

00:13:09.376 --> 00:13:12.244
Ariel Sanchez Mora asked me to.

00:13:12.244 --> 00:13:13.312
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:13.312 --> 00:13:14.984
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:14.984 --> 00:13:17.410
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:20.034
I'd also love to know which
of Tom Brady's top 10 rules

00:13:20.034 --> 00:13:22.449
meant the most to you,
had the biggest impact.

00:13:22.449 --> 00:13:24.167
Leave it in the comments,
I'll join in the discussion.

00:13:24.167 --> 00:13:25.816
That you so much for watching.

00:13:25.816 --> 00:13:28.521
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:13:28.521 --> 00:13:31.354
(dramatic swoosh)

00:13:34.599 --> 00:13:36.764
- Team sports are all about the team.

00:13:36.764 --> 00:13:39.063
And that was true as a
freshman here at Serra,

00:13:39.063 --> 00:13:43.150
and it still rings true into
my 13th year with the Patriots.

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:45.170
And as I first learned here, football,

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:48.084
and life for that matter,
is about competition.

00:13:48.084 --> 00:13:50.383
And I took my first ever reps here,

00:13:50.383 --> 00:13:54.179
for the freshman football team in 1991.

00:13:54.179 --> 00:13:56.675
And I competed with my
friend Kevin Krystofiak,

00:13:56.675 --> 00:13:59.856
who's here, to be the quarterback.

00:13:59.856 --> 00:14:02.887
And I lost the competition,
but I was excited

00:14:02.887 --> 00:14:04.489
to be a part of the team anyway.

00:14:04.489 --> 00:14:06.079
And I was second string
that year and learned

00:14:06.079 --> 00:14:08.866
from the reps that I got in practice.

00:14:08.866 --> 00:14:11.826
And I did get a chance
to get into a few games.

00:14:11.826 --> 00:14:14.996
I remember playing at Reardan,
it was late in the game,

00:14:14.996 --> 00:14:18.142
we were losing, and my number was called.

00:14:18.142 --> 00:14:20.685
And it was great to finally be playing.

00:14:20.685 --> 00:14:21.834
Until after a few plays,

00:14:21.834 --> 00:14:23.773
coach Joe Connor yelled from the sideline,

00:14:23.773 --> 00:14:28.196
"Brady, you look like you're
running in slow motion."

00:14:28.196 --> 00:14:29.311
(audience laughs)

00:14:29.311 --> 00:14:31.772
Which was true, then.

00:14:31.772 --> 00:14:33.908
And it's still true today.

00:14:33.908 --> 00:14:37.136
(audience laughs and applauds)

00:14:37.136 --> 00:14:38.136
I know, Mom.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:43.707
My mom still thinks I'm
the fastest one out there.

00:14:43.707 --> 00:14:46.250
(audience laughs)

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.917
She says, honey don't
say that, you're fast.

00:14:51.056 --> 00:14:53.936
But we went oh and eight that
year, and I hardly played.

00:14:53.936 --> 00:14:55.457
Not really the best start for someone

00:14:55.457 --> 00:14:58.162
who would end up making a
career playing the game.

00:14:58.162 --> 00:14:59.845
And as a 14 year old boy, I realized

00:14:59.845 --> 00:15:02.063
how much I loved the game.

00:15:02.063 --> 00:15:03.293
And just as importantly, I enjoyed

00:15:03.293 --> 00:15:05.755
the practice and the
preparation, working to improve,

00:15:05.755 --> 00:15:08.379
and I realized how much
improvement I needed to make

00:15:08.379 --> 00:15:10.956
in order to continue
playing past high school.

00:15:10.956 --> 00:15:14.139
So I worked, and I studied, and
I listened, and I practiced,

00:15:14.139 --> 00:15:15.694
and by my senior year I
was playing well enough

00:15:15.694 --> 00:15:19.368
to be recruited by a
few Division I schools.

00:15:19.368 --> 00:15:22.168
And after an intense recruiting
process I chose Michigan,

00:15:22.168 --> 00:15:24.792
I took my first steps onto
that playing field in college

00:15:24.792 --> 00:15:27.776
as the seventh quarterback on the team.

00:15:27.776 --> 00:15:30.202
And I was hoping that
one day I'd get a chance

00:15:30.202 --> 00:15:31.805
to lead the team on the Michigan stadium

00:15:31.805 --> 00:15:35.113
and play in front of 112,000 fans.

00:15:35.113 --> 00:15:38.074
But it was pretty clear it
wasn't going to happen overnight.

00:15:38.074 --> 00:15:40.291
So there were over 120
practices my first year,

00:15:40.291 --> 00:15:43.031
not to mention the hundreds
of hours of meetings

00:15:43.031 --> 00:15:47.188
we had in order to prepare us
to play Big 10 competition.

00:15:47.188 --> 00:15:48.175
And it all seemed to pay off

00:15:48.175 --> 00:15:50.381
when I first got my chance to play.

00:15:50.381 --> 00:15:52.517
And I was so excited, I had all my friends

00:15:52.517 --> 00:15:54.757
from home watching, maybe some of you.

00:15:54.757 --> 00:15:56.650
My parents were in the crowd,

00:15:56.650 --> 00:16:00.133
we were ahead with a
huge lead against UCLA.

00:16:00.133 --> 00:16:02.559
I was put into the game
in the fourth quarter,

00:16:02.559 --> 00:16:04.603
and the coach called
my favorite pass play.

00:16:04.603 --> 00:16:07.122
So I dropped back, trying
to impress the coach,

00:16:07.122 --> 00:16:09.572
and I looked off the safety a little bit,

00:16:09.572 --> 00:16:11.464
and I fired the ball right
over the middle of the field.

00:16:11.464 --> 00:16:14.611
I hit the player right in stride,
right between the numbers,

00:16:14.611 --> 00:16:16.712
and he ran 45 yards for a touchdown.

00:16:16.712 --> 00:16:18.558
No one touched him.

00:16:18.558 --> 00:16:23.446
Just as I had dreamed, the
crowd erupted, I was horrified.

00:16:23.446 --> 00:16:26.023
That was UCLA's only points of the game.

00:16:26.023 --> 00:16:30.203
(audience laughs and applauds)

00:16:30.203 --> 00:16:34.231
A 45 yard interception
return for a touchdown.

00:16:34.231 --> 00:16:37.238
Welcome to college football.

00:16:37.238 --> 00:16:40.849
I wasn't sure my coach
would ever put me in again.

00:16:40.849 --> 00:16:43.055
But we all have experiences
that we dream about,

00:16:43.055 --> 00:16:44.495
and that we look forward to.

00:16:44.495 --> 00:16:47.606
And when we get them, they
don't always go our way.

00:16:47.606 --> 00:16:50.601
I learned that as a freshman
on the football field

00:16:50.601 --> 00:16:52.320
just outside of this gym.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.166
And again I learned it
as a second year player

00:16:54.166 --> 00:16:57.486
who finally got a chance
to play in college.

00:16:57.486 --> 00:16:59.364
Unfortunately I learned
it three weeks ago,

00:16:59.364 --> 00:17:02.684
in our loss to the
Giants in Super Bowl 46.

00:17:02.684 --> 00:17:04.411
But I've always been
blessed with the support

00:17:04.411 --> 00:17:06.536
of my family and friends,
and also with coaches

00:17:06.536 --> 00:17:09.659
that committed their best to me.

00:17:09.659 --> 00:17:11.540
And with each opportunity,
came an understanding

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:14.268
of the things that it
takes to be successful.

00:17:14.268 --> 00:17:17.054
And often this happens
through trial and error,

00:17:17.054 --> 00:17:19.388
and sometimes through outright failure.

00:17:19.388 --> 00:17:22.685
Because Lord knows, I
made plenty of mistakes.

00:17:22.685 --> 00:17:24.647
So whether I was learning
through watching,

00:17:24.647 --> 00:17:26.356
as I did my freshman year at Serra,

00:17:26.356 --> 00:17:29.433
or playing and making mistakes
over the course of my career,

00:17:29.433 --> 00:17:32.336
I've continued to learn
to embrace every hardship.

00:17:32.336 --> 00:17:35.180
And to seize every opportunity
on and off the field.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.220
The owner of my current
team, Robert Kraft,

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.387
has told me time and time
again, every bump is a bounce.

00:17:42.398 --> 00:17:44.648
So what I learned very
quickly as a professional,

00:17:44.648 --> 00:17:47.353
was that your job is always on the line.

00:17:47.353 --> 00:17:52.171
I'm in a what have you done
for me lately, type business.

00:17:52.171 --> 00:17:54.098
As in all businesses,
there are disclosures.

00:17:54.098 --> 00:17:56.037
In investments it says, past performance

00:17:56.037 --> 00:17:58.045
doesn't guarantee future results.

00:17:58.045 --> 00:17:59.613
(audience laughs)

00:17:59.613 --> 00:18:02.399
well that pretty much sums up the NFL.

00:18:02.399 --> 00:18:03.665
And there are always going to be younger

00:18:03.665 --> 00:18:07.450
and hungrier athletes
competing to take your spot.

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:09.864
I got my opportunity when
incumbent starter Drew Bledsoe

00:18:09.864 --> 00:18:11.954
got injured my second year.

00:18:11.954 --> 00:18:14.125
And all of the practices and meetings,

00:18:14.125 --> 00:18:18.142
and time spent on these
fields, and in this gym,

00:18:18.142 --> 00:18:19.942
and in those classrooms, prepared me

00:18:19.942 --> 00:18:22.113
to take advantage of the opportunity.

00:18:22.113 --> 00:18:24.481
And 10 years later, after
great success in my career,

00:18:24.481 --> 00:18:26.827
I wouldn't change a day in my life.

00:18:26.827 --> 00:18:30.597
- He was skinny, slow (laughs), right?

00:18:30.597 --> 00:18:32.896
Too pretty to be a football player.

00:18:32.896 --> 00:18:35.113
But he was smarter than everyone.

00:18:35.113 --> 00:18:37.818
And he studied night
and day, day and night.

00:18:37.818 --> 00:18:40.901
- Nobody works harder than Tom Brady.

00:18:42.021 --> 00:18:44.587
- If you didn't play well on Tuesday

00:18:44.587 --> 00:18:46.897
and Wednesday and Thursday
in practice in college,

00:18:46.897 --> 00:18:49.312
I didn't know if I was
going to start the game.

00:18:49.312 --> 00:18:51.599
So when I come to the
Patriots, that's how I treat

00:18:51.599 --> 00:18:53.364
Wednesday and Thursday and Friday.

00:18:53.364 --> 00:18:54.478
- [Coach] That's it, that's it.

00:18:54.478 --> 00:18:57.543
Nice patience on that, nice patience.

00:18:57.543 --> 00:19:00.043
- Let's go, let's go, let's go

00:19:03.913 --> 00:19:04.746
Ready, hut

00:19:05.618 --> 00:19:08.567
(dramatic music)

00:19:08.567 --> 00:19:10.784
(crowd cheering)

00:19:10.784 --> 00:19:12.867
(grunts)

00:19:15.254 --> 00:19:16.609
- [Announcer] Stands
in, stands in, steps up

00:19:16.609 --> 00:19:20.776
looks to the right, throws
to the end zone, touchdown.

00:19:21.851 --> 00:19:25.848
- Hey, we ain't done fellas,
we ain't done, this ain't over.

00:19:25.848 --> 00:19:28.399
Come on you all we ain't
done, we talked about that.

00:19:28.399 --> 00:19:30.945
We got to start competing, start
playing with fire all of us.

00:19:30.945 --> 00:19:35.635
Hey, come on now fellas, we got
to answer a bell now let's go

00:19:35.635 --> 00:19:38.004
(dramatic music)

00:19:38.004 --> 00:19:40.244
- [Announcer] Looking,
firing end zone, touchdown.

00:19:40.244 --> 00:19:42.740
(crowd cheering)

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:44.494
And now Anthony Smith and Tom Brady

00:19:44.494 --> 00:19:45.968
had some words at the end of that,

00:19:45.968 --> 00:19:47.884
I guess Tom Brady's saying,

00:19:47.884 --> 00:19:49.764
"Hey, you guarantee that win, or what?"

00:19:49.764 --> 00:19:52.133
- Come on guys, too many mistakes.

00:19:52.133 --> 00:19:54.942
Hey, we got to be perfect, alright.

00:19:54.942 --> 00:19:57.961
Hey, take it to another level, alright.

00:19:57.961 --> 00:20:01.398
Come on baby, come on fellas, come on.

00:20:01.398 --> 00:20:04.731
We got to fight, or
we're going to lay down.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:12.601
I hate sitting on the bench
man, I hate sitting on the bench

00:20:12.601 --> 00:20:15.351
(dramatic music)

00:20:18.035 --> 00:20:21.587
- [Announcer] Tom Brady, just won't quit.

00:20:21.587 --> 00:20:23.143
- [Announcer] Quarterback sneak by Brady,

00:20:23.143 --> 00:20:25.060
and he's in, touchdown.

00:20:26.406 --> 00:20:29.471
- [Announcer] Tom Brady,
quarterback sneak, touchdown

00:20:29.471 --> 00:20:30.554
- Yeah! Yeah!

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:38.387
- [Announcer] Good evening
everyone, and welcome to

00:20:38.387 --> 00:20:40.812
the winter wonderland
of this Foxboro Stadium.

00:20:40.812 --> 00:20:43.638
Tonight the Patriots
against the Oakland Raiders.

00:20:43.638 --> 00:20:48.529
- [Tom] We're going to get this
one, I promise, I promise that.

00:20:48.529 --> 00:20:50.137
- [Announcer] Second and goal
to go at the Raider's six.

00:20:50.137 --> 00:20:53.016
The snap to Brady, looks,
stands, starts to run with it.

00:20:53.016 --> 00:20:54.862
He is down to the five, and he's in.

00:20:54.862 --> 00:20:56.612
Touchdown, Tom Brady.

00:20:57.973 --> 00:21:00.063
- [Announcer] And he gets
up and spikes the ball

00:21:00.063 --> 00:21:02.563
and goes wild here at Foxboro.

00:21:04.219 --> 00:21:08.340
- Yeah baby, we got some great comebacks.

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:09.915
Good job man.

00:21:09.915 --> 00:21:12.131
- [Announcer] Direct snap
to Brady, standing in there,

00:21:12.131 --> 00:21:13.768
looks to the left, loses the football.

00:21:13.768 --> 00:21:16.066
It's on the ground, a fumble,

00:21:16.066 --> 00:21:19.108
and the Oakland Raiders recover,
with a minute and 43 to go.

00:21:19.108 --> 00:21:22.289
- [Referee] After reviewing
the play, the quarterback's arm

00:21:22.289 --> 00:21:24.588
was going forward it is an inc--

00:21:24.588 --> 00:21:28.831
- [Announcer] Oh look,
Patriots retain the ball.

00:21:28.831 --> 00:21:31.040
- [Announcer] Snap, ball down, kick is up

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:34.570
(crowd and team cheering)

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:39.564
- [Announcer] Patriots,
win in overtime, 16 to 13.

00:21:39.564 --> 00:21:43.564
(excited chatter among players)

00:21:45.834 --> 00:21:49.417
(yelling enthusiastically)

00:21:55.437 --> 00:21:58.154
- [Announcer] Super Bowl 36 is underway.

00:21:58.154 --> 00:22:00.325
From here in New Orleans.

00:22:00.325 --> 00:22:02.752
Brady first and goal, St.
Louis eight yard line.

00:22:02.752 --> 00:22:05.796
Brady drops back to
throw, looks, fires right.

00:22:05.796 --> 00:22:10.312
Touchdown David Patten,
touchdown David Patten.

00:22:10.312 --> 00:22:12.553
(crowd cheering)

00:22:12.553 --> 00:22:16.976
- [Announcer] First and 10
Patriots, at their own 17.

00:22:16.976 --> 00:22:19.275
- [Announcer] Tom Brady, overrated?

00:22:19.275 --> 00:22:20.529
- [Announcer] Alright folks, button up

00:22:20.529 --> 00:22:22.050
your chinstrap, here we go.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:23.071
- [Announcer] Stands up in the pocket,

00:22:23.071 --> 00:22:24.708
starts to run with it,
throws over the middle,

00:22:24.708 --> 00:22:27.657
it is caught there at the 21 yard line.

00:22:27.657 --> 00:22:29.457
Pulse pounding finish to this one here.

00:22:29.457 --> 00:22:31.303
Clock running to 1:09 to play.

00:22:31.303 --> 00:22:32.719
Over the middle of Jones,

00:22:32.719 --> 00:22:36.023
complete to J.R. Redmond
at the 30 yard line.

00:22:36.023 --> 00:22:39.159
41 seconds left, stands
in the pocket, steps up,

00:22:39.159 --> 00:22:42.700
fires down the field,
caught, Troy Brown at the 40.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:45.568
Looks, fires over the middle,
caught by Jermaine Wiggins,

00:22:45.568 --> 00:22:48.984
down to the 30 yard line of St. Louis.

00:22:48.984 --> 00:22:51.473
Throws the ball to the ground
with seven seconds left.

00:22:51.473 --> 00:22:53.040
Adam Vinatieri will try to win it

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:56.117
with a 47 yard field goal attempt.

00:22:56.117 --> 00:22:57.429
(dramatic music)

00:22:57.429 --> 00:22:59.611
Set to go, snap, ball down, kick up,

00:22:59.611 --> 00:23:01.944
kick is on the way and it is

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.190
good, it's good, it's good.

00:23:08.435 --> 00:23:12.498
Adam Vinatieri pulls a 48 yard field goal,

00:23:12.498 --> 00:23:14.925
and the game is over, and the Patriots

00:23:14.925 --> 00:23:17.456
are Super Bowl champions.

00:23:17.456 --> 00:23:21.373
The Patriots are Super
Bowl champions, oh baby.

00:23:22.982 --> 00:23:26.570
- Way to go 12, you the
man, baby, you are the man.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:28.416
- [Announcer] Well, New England you have

00:23:28.416 --> 00:23:32.249
the best football team
in the world right now.

00:23:35.602 --> 00:23:39.176
- We're going to Disney World (cheering)

